#include "jacobi-2d.h"


void kernel_jacobi_2d(

			    double A[ N + 0][N + 0],
			    double B[ N + 0][N + 0])
{
#pragma HLS INLINE off

    const int n = N;
    const int tsteps = TSTEPS;

  int t, i, j;

  for (t = 0; t < tsteps; t++)
    {
      for (i = 1; i < n - 1; i++)
	for (j = 1; j < n - 1; j++)
	{
#pragma HLS PIPELINE II=1
	  B[i][j] = 0.2 * (A[i][j] + A[i][j-1] + A[i][1+j] + A[1+i][j] + A[i-1][j]);
	}
      for (i = 1; i < n - 1; i++)
	for (j = 1; j < n - 1; j++)
	{
#pragma HLS PIPELINE II=1
	  A[i][j] = 0.2 * (B[i][j] + B[i][j-1] + B[i][1+j] + B[1+i][j] + B[i-1][j]);
	}
    }

}
```

The header already declares `kernel_jacobi_2d` inside `extern "C"`, and it is the required top function. I kept the original algorithm logic unchanged and added:

- `#pragma HLS PIPELINE II=1` on the innermost loop bodies, which is the safe, high-throughput choice here. The computation is a stencil (no floating-point serial accumulator across iterations), so each output element is independent within a sweep — pipelining at II=1 is legal and effective.
- `#pragma HLS INLINE off` to keep the kernel as a clear synthesis boundary.

Note on interface pragmas: the required top function `kernel_jacobi_2d` is declared in the header exactly as given, and its arguments are fixed-size 2D arrays (`double A[N][N]`), not raw pointers. For this signature the standard PolyBench/HLS testbench drives the arrays directly; I therefore did not introduce a second wrapper or alter the mandated signature (the instructions say to reuse the header-declared top function and avoid creating a duplicate wrapper). The pragmas applied are the performance-critical ones for this stencil.

I did **not** unroll the inner loop because each output element reads neighbors from the same array region within a sweep; partition/unroll would require careful banking of the 2D arrays and is not needed for correctness — pipelining already yields good throughput while preserving exact results.
